        "id": "stage-2-topic-5-section-9",
        "title": "Pull Request Lifecycle — Security and Governance",
        "content": "# Pull Request Lifecycle — Security and Governance\n\n## 1. Explanation and Learning Objective\n\nThe section **Pull Request Lifecycle — Security and Governance** belongs to the navigation topic **Pull Request Lifecycle** in Stage 02: Pull Request and Merge.\n\nThis topic concerns the controlled review of proposed source-code changes. A pull request compares a source branch with a target branch and gives reviewers a place to inspect changed files, discuss implementation decisions, request corrections, and verify automated checks. The review must consider correctness, readability, security, test coverage, backward compatibility, and operational impact.\n\nExplain the pull-request lifecycle from publishing a source branch to final approval. Cover the source and target branches, title, description, changed-file diff, commit list, reviewer assignment, inline comments, requested changes, approvals, and required status checks. Explain how authors should respond to feedback with corrective commits and how reviewers verify that the correction actually addresses the concern. Include the importance of keeping the description aligned with the real implementation.\n\nBy completing this section, an engineer should be able to explain the concept in plain English, identify the Git or GitHub components involved, perform the relevant operation, inspect its result, and recognize the risks of using it incorrectly.\n\n## 2. Technical Behavior and Repository State\n\nGit is a distributed version-control system. A local repository stores project content and commit history. The working tree contains the files currently present on disk. The staging index records the content selected for the next commit. A commit stores a snapshot and links to its parent commit or commits.\n\nA branch is a movable reference to a commit. A remote such as origin identifies another repository. Remote-tracking references represent the last fetched state of remote branches. GitHub adds hosted