        "id": "stage-2-topic-9-section-8",
        "title": "Merge Conflict Resolution — Conflict Prevention and Resolution",
        "content": "# Merge Conflict Resolution — Conflict Prevention and Resolution\n\n## 1. Explanation and Learning Objective\n\nThe section **Merge Conflict Resolution — Conflict Prevention and Resolution** belongs to the navigation topic **Merge Conflict Resolution** in Stage 02: Pull Request and Merge.\n\nThis topic concerns combining independent development histories. Git compares branch histories and attempts to integrate compatible changes. A merge commit, squash merge, or rebase produces different history structures. Conflict resolution requires understanding both implementations and verifying that the final combined code preserves the required behavior.\n\nExplain how Git integrates the source history into the target history. Compare a merge commit, squash merge, and rebase, including their effect on commit topology and traceability. Explain fast-forward conditions, non-fast-forward history, merge-base calculation, and why a team's repository policy should determine the selected method. Include checks that should be performed before and after integration.\n\nBy completing this section, an engineer should be able to explain the concept in plain English, identify the Git or GitHub components involved, perform the relevant operation, inspect its result, and recognize the risks of using it incorrectly.\n\n## 2. Technical Behavior and Repository State\n\nGit is a distributed version-control system. A local repository stores project content and commit history. The working tree contains the files currently present on disk. The staging index records the content selected for the next commit. A commit stores a snapshot and links to its parent commit or commits.\n\nA branch is a movable reference to a commit. A remote such as origin identifies another repository. Remote-tracking references represent the last fetched state of remote branches. GitHub adds hosted collaboration features such as pull requests, review conversations, branch protection, required checks, repository permissions, and audit history.\n\nFor **Merge Conflict Resolution — Conflict Prevention and Resolution**, the engineer must identify which of these components is involved and what state is expected before and after the operation. Do not assume that a local branch, remote branch, pull request, and merged commit are interchangeable. They represent different parts of the collaboration lifecycle.\n\n## 3. Implementation Workflow\n\n1. Confirm the repository directory and verify that it is the intended project.\n2. Run git status and inspect the current branch before making changes.\n3. Review the relevant commit history and configured remote.\n4. Confirm the source branch and target branch for the intended change.\n5. Perform the operation described by this section using the team's approved procedure.\n6. Inspect the resulting diff, branch state, and command output.\n7. Run relevant unit tests, integration tests, build checks, and security checks.\n8. Publish or integrate the change only after required review and validation conditions are satisfied.\n9. Record the resulting commit or pull-request reference so the change can be traced later.\n\nThe sequence matters because Git commands operate on repository state. Running a merge, reset, rebase, or push without checking the current branch can affect the wrong code or publish changes to an unintended destination.\n\n## 4. Practical Git Commands\n\nUse the commands relevant to this section. Replace example branch names, file names, and commit IDs with values from the actual repository.\n\n`\bash\ngit status\ngit fetch origin\ngit log --graph --oneline --decorate --all\ngit merge origin/main\ngit diff\ngit add <resolved-file>\ngit merge --continue\ngit log --graph --oneline -10\n`\n\nBefore running a command that changes history or remote state, inspect its purpose and expected effect. Commands such as reset, rebase, force-push, and branch deletion require additional care when work has already been shared.\n\n## 5. Production DevOps Example\n\nConsider a Java-based payment service maintained by several developers. One engineer implements payment amount validation while another updates transaction logging. Both changes must reach the protected main branch without losing either implementation.\n\nThe first engineer works on a dedicated feature branch and commits only the related source and test files. The branch is pushed to the hosted repository and submitted for review. Reviewers inspect the implementation, test coverage, error handling, security implications, and compatibility with existing callers.\n\nThe CI workflow checks out the proposed revision, installs the required JDK, restores build dependencies, runs the Maven test suite, and reports its result. If a required check fails, the pull request must not be treated as ready to merge. The author investigates the failure, pushes a correction, and waits for the checks to complete again.\n\nAfter approval, the change is integrated using the repository's configured merge strategy. The team verifies the final target-branch commit and confirms that the expected downstream build or deployment pipeline has started.\n\n## 6. Common Mistakes and Their Impact\n\n- Performing an operation while on the wrong branch can change or publish unintended code.\n- Using an outdated target branch can introduce avoidable conflicts or omit important fixes.\n- Reviewing only the pull-request description instead of the actual diff can allow defects to pass.\n- Treating a successful local test as proof that all CI checks passed can hide environment-specific failures.\n- Resolving conflicts by blindly accepting one side can remove valid changes from another developer.\n- Force-pushing shared history can disrupt other developers and invalidate their local branch assumptions.\n- Committing credentials or generated files can expose secrets and pollute the repository.\n- Merging without recording the final commit makes release investigation and rollback more difficult.\n\n## 7. Troubleshooting and Safe Recovery\n\nStart with git status, the current branch, the remote configuration, and recent commit history. Capture the exact error message instead of repeatedly retrying the same command.\n\nFor a rejected push, fetch the remote branch and inspect the commits that differ before choosing a merge or rebase strategy. For a merge conflict, inspect both versions and the common context, edit the intended final implementation, stage the resolved files, and run tests. For an authentication failure, verify the configured credential method and repository permissions without printing secrets into logs.\n\nIf a change has already been shared, prefer a corrective commit or git revert when appropriate instead of rewriting shared history. Preserve uncommitted work before using destructive recovery commands.\n\n## 8. Verification and Completion Criteria\n\nThe operation is complete only when the repository state matches the intended result.\n\nVerify the following:\n\n- The correct repository and branch were used.\n- The intended files and commits are included.\n- Unrelated files and accidental changes are not included.\n- No unresolved conflict markers remain.\n- Relevant tests and build checks have passed.\n- Required pull-request approvals and status checks are satisfied.\n- The target branch contains the expected integrated change.\n- The final commit can be traced to the review and validation process.\n\n## 9. DevOps Engineering Best Practices\n\nKeep changes small and reviewable. Use descriptive branch names and commit messages. Protect important target branches. Require automated checks for critical repositories. Use least-privilege credentials and never store secrets in source control. Keep feature branches synchronized using the team's documented policy.\n\nFor production systems, connect the merged commit to the build artifact and deployment record. This allows engineers to identify exactly which source revision is running, investigate incidents, and reproduce a release when required.\n\n## 10. Interview and Practical Understanding\n\nAn engineer should be able to explain what **Merge Conflict Resolution — Conflict Prevention and Resolution** means, why the team needs it, how Git or GitHub implements it, which commands or interface actions are involved, what can go wrong, and how the result is verified.\n\nA strong practical answer describes the repository state before the operation, the exact steps performed, the evidence collected, and the final state. Avoid saying only that a command was executed. Explain what the command changed and how that change was confirmed."
      },
      {
